## Verdict

**READY WITH NAMED CONDITIONS.** The evidence-prosecution pass carried the draft past its arithmetic problems. The argument now holds. What still fails the executive test is the operational, legal, and calendar architecture around the recommendations. Seven fatal items — the legal identity of the register (F-1), the unilateral-authority assumption (F-2), the missing dates (F-3), the six-way first-90-day list (F-4), the CBP staffing assumption (F-5), the external-publication recommendation (F-6), and the "fund the second bill" placeholder (F-7) — have to be resolved before this reaches the Board. Thirteen implementation conditions (C-8 through C-20) have to be attached to the recommendations that survive. Each of the five decision cards has to be completed in the format above.

The core thesis — generic hub geometry, priced options, carrier-funded conversion, register as governance instrument — is right. The report can go to the executive team and the airline-properties table now. It cannot go to the Board until it names who signs what, on what authority, funded from where, on what date, with what consequence when the date slips.
